      "content": "i mean, i get the \"borderline\" thing, but if i say \"borderline significant\" to the investors, they're gonna grill me on the math. it's basically a no-go if it doesn't hit the 0.05 mark. \n\ncan we just focus on the effect size or the direction of the data? i want to avoid using the phrase \"not statistically significant\" or even \"borderline\" if we can help it. can we write this so it sounds like we've already cleared the hurdle of proving it works, just without explicitly stating the p-value? something that implies the efficacy is already established so they don't question the 0.06?",
